#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088745} :activity/id #uuid "d16c173d-d5fc-51c9-adf4-fe3999473ab4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622 :topic/id #uuid "0613a6d7-8b7b-5bc2-9f9a-e72e48c2994a" :topic/math-academy-id 814} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088990 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our first example once more.\n\n![](images/bc/bc15538fc507a42e4ef0cde0798d90cafca6d3d02e5e7c1630c6fc3e4a1e306b.png)\n\nYou might be wondering how we can tell that the vertex coordinates are ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) just from looking at the equation. \n\nTo see this, let's label the two terms in our parabola formula:\n\n![](images/03/03e67590d6a4c3f9257b2330fcce119e3710397453b36fb0f9486964259ba40b.svg)\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The quadratic part ![](images/36/3658549ec19c3c38654ef48e3982b82008b2630ebd0de7f5fe04bd7d4d7073ef.svg) is a *squared* quantity. Therefore, it can only be *positive* or *zero*.\n\n- Moreover, the quadratic part *equals* zero when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg)\n\n- Since the constant part ![](images/07/07e82ee914d3f32c77556663cbc38417dc5f057c180e58a1fb4230bb364f5c25.svg) does not vary with ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) the entire expression is *minimized* when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of the minimum point (i.e., the vertex) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n- When ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) the quadratic part is zero, giving ![](images/21/2182a4d19eb1c758d967edc4951247e3d512519ac6d8b95676bc51eb2fe57d1c.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate of the vertex is ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg)\n\nFollowing this logic, we conclude that the coordinates of the vertex are ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\nFinally, it's possible to generalize this argument to any parabola. For ![](images/28/28e6012b2b10c0a7f40681ab8a73666a12afd6e976c59f536ed30d1defca5f64.svg) the coordinates ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) give the maximum of the parabola." :tutorial/id #uuid "d30b5d5a-0248-511a-9f7f-dfff00438889" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9115 :tutorial/title "Why Does Vertex Form Give the Coordinates of the Vertex?"} :step/id #uuid "c203d163-0475-523c-b583-dea9d27a9184" :step/math-academy-id 136678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087631 :question/id #uuid "d4c1e4ea-da29-5027-bfc3-f909f9181cd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97f63d2a-8297-5034-96e0-ecae4f62c5a8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562506}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum or Maximum Value of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "a04762c2-835a-5511-af4c-a7041c880b62" :step/math-academy-id 27764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088870 :tutorial/content "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n![](images/e3/e3fb2f9598362bc373e475faf19bb84623a73281da549bf85018f22b7f8a095d.svg)\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n![](images/4d/4dc70525f24857744b03c32f2f558b27691c823f17bab16a569d3bbdadab732b.svg)\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola: \n\n![](images/23/2366bd4f3ea2bcb500a85b13c29a3b512d0529ffc82e507c81dae3df96176981.svg)\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with ![](images/b4/b4edfee225234d67bf8c5a1f8283fe3eb5edba258f6268e9ffe7e604def55858.svg) and ![](images/6f/6fa7648f1eeb4effdf9b60cf5199b0ac26e1c1d57c0f05d9e9bd895e5c277c21.svg) Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\n![](images/4e/4e050c9f52965d9682cae055c1264ac2db9a58b09557f6e15f0178e9375b3872.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7456ef9-dd1e-56e5-a24f-111ba9692968" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1648 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbd152b4-52af-56c9-8eab-5981824f4582" :step/math-academy-id 4592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087436 :question/id #uuid "c9e4edff-b04f-5f6c-8066-43cae6a653e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9b29c-326d-576c-b998-ab975492ed85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049919}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562504}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of 